    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one more thing though, the client is really worried about the kitchen staff getting overwhelmed or accidentally mixing things up during the rush. do you have any tips for preventing those accidental \"hidden\" errors, like if something is cooked in the same pan or if a garnish is added at the last second? something that's not just a checklist but more of a foolproof system?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Implementing color-coded chrome trays for each menu station can physically separate allergen-free items from those containing nuts, preventing cross-contamination during the rushed plating phase. Consider designating specific \"safe\" prep stations in the kitchen with dedicated equipment, ensuring that even garnishes and sauces are prepared and stored separately to avoid last-minute mixing errors. Some high-end firms also use a \"touch cross-check\" protocol where every plate leaving the kitchen must be verbally cleared by a second staff member before serving begins. This dual-layer approach of physical separation and human verification acts as a robust safety net beyond standard checklists.",
      "source": "target",
